Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever a refund amount is calculated and is not a whole number of rupees, round it down to the whole rupee, in the shop's favour (e.g. ₹833.50 becomes ₹833).
  2. Whenever an order that earned loyalty points is later refunded, in full or in part, the customer keeps the points already earned. The refund does not remove any points.
  3. Whenever a customer requests a refund, count the days since delivery, treating a negative number as day 0. If the goods are damaged, a request made up to and including day 90 gets the full amount. If the goods are not damaged and the order is not final-sale: a request made up to and including day 14 gets the full amount (e.g. day 9 gets a full refund); a request made from day 15 through day 30 gets half the amount; a request made after day 30 gets nothing.
  4. Whenever a refund is calculated, the base is the item prices times quantities minus the discount code amount, which is what the customer actually paid for the items (e.g. ₹1,200 item with a ₹200 coupon gives a base of ₹1,000). The day rules are applied to that base. The shipping fee (e.g. ₹100) is not refunded, except when the goods are damaged, in which case the shipping fee is refunded as well. This applies to repeat refunds too.
  5. Whenever the calculated refund is under ₹50, it is not paid, unless the goods are damaged.
  6. Whenever a customer has already received refunds on an order, the total refunded across all requests can never exceed what he paid for the items. A new request can receive at most the remaining amount (e.g. ₹500 already refunded on a ₹2,000 order leaves at most ₹1,500).
  7. Whenever a refund is paid, send it back the way the order was paid: orders paid with store credit are refunded as store credit, and orders paid by card are refunded to the card.
  8. Whenever an order is paid by card, the customer earns 1 loyalty point for every full ₹100 of the item total after the discount code is taken off, ignoring any remaining part-hundred (e.g. ₹1,990 gives 19 points). Shipping earns no points. If the item total after the discount is under ₹500, the order earns no points. Final-sale orders earn double points. An order paid with store credit earns no points.
  9. Whenever the daily summary counts orders, it counts every order in the list (paid, cancelled, unpaid, refunded fully or partly, card or store credit) with no exclusions, so 15 orders in the list count as 15 orders.
  10. Whenever the daily summary calculates revenue, it counts only money that came in: card orders only, each as its items after discount plus shipping. Store-credit orders add zero revenue. Refunds do not change the summary.
  11. Whenever the daily report shows the average per order, it is the day's revenue divided by the number of card orders only, rounded down to a whole rupee (e.g. 3 orders totalling ₹1,000 gives ₹333). If the day has no orders or no card orders, the average is ₹0. A day with no orders at all shows 0 orders, ₹0 revenue and ₹0 average.
  12. When an order is final-sale and the goods are not damaged, and the request comes from day 15 through day 30 after delivery, what refund does the customer get? -> B) Nothing. Final-sale orders get no refund unless the goods arrived damaged, whatever the day.
